WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.860
Narrator: What you're seeing
here isn't a real cockroach.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.685
[dramatic music]

00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:07.970
[crunching]

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:10.360
It's actually a cleverly disguised date,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.510
a trick Melissa McSorley mastered

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.710
for a scene from "Crazy Ex-Girlfriend"

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.430
where a character picks
up a live cockroach

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.850
and eats it whole.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.590
It's an opportunity to do something

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.660
that's out of the normal realm for me,

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.185
and it gives me an
opportunity to start coming up

00:00:25.185 --> 00:00:26.343
with ways to tackle it.

00:00:26.343 --> 00:00:28.240
Narrator: Most props are
meant to be picked up

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.810
and handled, but some
need to be some consumed.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.390
That's where food stylists
like Melissa come in

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.700
and transform seaweed and chile threads

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.360
into cockroach legs and antennae.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.060
So how can she make
everything from live bugs

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.860
to guts to blood edible?

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.052
And do they actually taste good?

00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:48.440
[crunching]

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.820
The seaweed crunch, right?

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:50.653
That's really smart

00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:51.694
'cause it's going to make crunchy --

00:00:51.694 --> 00:00:53.386
you got to get them to crunch.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.410
Narrator: Food stylists
are used to solve problems

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.370
like making sure ice cream doesn't melt

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.620
or finding a convincing
substitute for runny yolks.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.440
When it comes to replicating things

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.080
one normally wouldn't eat,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.820
Melissa has to use edible ingredients

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.350
that create the same look
as the nonedible prop.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.980
Like blood.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.540
Typical fake human blood isn't toxic,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.830
and an actor can even have
a little around their mouth.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.620
But when Melissa worked on "True Blood,"

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.960
she needed something safe
enough for vampire characters

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.050
to drink in large amounts.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.330
She started with a mix of
Cran-Cherry and pomegranate juice,

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.110
great for scenes where a glass or bottle

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.180
was just sitting out or being held.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.140
But she also had to replicate
the true properties of blood,

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.420
like its opaqueness,

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.160
which is why she added
wheatgrass to her formula.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.590
And there isn't just red
food coloring in there.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:41.593
Melissa: I had to add a little blue

00:01:41.593 --> 00:01:44.370
because these juices were far more red

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.740
than blood would normally be.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.810
So I just had to tamp it down a little.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.220
Narrator: As a former phlebotomist,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.220
Melissa knew that these small details

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.580
would add to the realism,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.550
so she also focused on the
viscosity of the blood.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.776
If it was a scene where you would see them

00:01:57.776 --> 00:02:00.340
pick up the glass and
drink and put it down,

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.740
you would want to see the
blood adhere to the side

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.450
and slowly go down.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.740
Narrator: To achieve this effect,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.767
she thickened her concoction
with methylcellulose.

00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:10.743
Melissa: And then if
someone were to drink it,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.000
we can see that it will slide back down

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.480
the side of the glass.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.313
Cheers.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.593
This is really good.

00:02:24.593 --> 00:02:27.620
This is like, yeah, it tastes
just like a really fine juice

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.573
'cause it's so thick.

00:02:28.573 --> 00:02:29.780
If I was on the "True Blood" set,

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:30.613
they'd have to -- they'd be like,

00:02:30.613 --> 00:02:32.460
"Stop drinking that.
Drink something else."

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.350
Narrator: Sometimes
productions want an edible prop

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.660
even if the actors aren't
planning on eating it,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.430
like this scene from the
Amazon series "Homecoming."

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:43.100
These two characters would
be handling raw chicken guts

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.890
and other foods and
objects at the same time.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.930
To avoid cross-contamination on set,

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.440
Melissa created edible
and safe chicken guts

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.730
out of carefully disguised food.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.570
While sausage casings made
sense for the intestines,

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.380
meat can easily go bad
during long shoot days.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.120
So instead, she filled collagen
casings with bean curd.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.880
Chicken blood is also more yellow,

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.530
so she dyed her ingredients yellow

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.730
and then switched to pink

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.030
as the intestines grew pinker
the further down they go.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.440
It's important to use foods

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.000
that can take many
different shapes and sizes.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.840
For example, Melissa would use
ramen or glass noodles here

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.430
over spaghetti, as they are more pliable.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:22.360
Melissa: It takes on different
shapes than spaghetti does.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.330
It also has different edges to it.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.320
Where spaghetti's  totally round,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.840
this is probably more like a linguine.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.570
Narrator: Then, by dyeing
these turnips different colors,

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.650
they could stand in for both
the heart and the liver.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.030
On top of all that,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.420
you can see cherry tomatoes and kumquats.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.530
This is the kind of detail
that could only come

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.410
from the close eye of a food stylist.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.400
So, while I was doing my research,

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.360
I found a few pictures of hens

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.550
that actually had unlaid
eggs inside their cavity.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:55.910
And they basically look
like a very, very orange

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.100
organic egg yolk.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.080
Narrator: If Melissa
can't find a specific food

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.760
that can stand in for something else,

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.360
she'll have to make her
edible props from scratch.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.860
Like in this scene from the
Netflix series "Daybreak"

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.620
where Ms. Crumble eats a
handful of live maggots

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.930
pulled from the trash.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.250
In this shot, you see actual live maggots

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.000
who were onset with the wrangler
and had to be kept safe.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.410
So in this shot,

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:20.970
the maggot actors were swapped
for fake gummy maggots.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:21.960
Well, I mean, I know they're fake,

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.640
but, I mean, they look
exactly like maggots.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.000
So there's a part of me
that's just like, "Eugh!"

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.763
You know, especially when
I look down the bowl,

00:04:27.763 --> 00:04:29.820
you got a couple stuck to the side there.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.120
Narrator: To get the correct form,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.080
Melissa started off with
a mold of plastic grubs

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.500
that she found at a bait-and-tackle store.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.430
And they were very, very
close in  size and shape,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.140
and they had the same
ribbing as a little maggot.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.610
Narrator: She filled
the mold with gelatin,

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.460
which made them naturally wiggle.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:46.750
More movement can be added

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.923
based on how you hold
the bowl full of them.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.729
Oh, so it's just -- they start --

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:52.609
It's just -- they literally --

00:04:52.609 --> 00:04:53.900
Joe: So they kind of start cascading down.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:54.733
Melissa: Yeah.

00:04:54.733 --> 00:04:55.910
Narrator: Even though they were so tiny,

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:57.840
they couldn't be uniform in color.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.110
Melissa: Over the course
of the life of a maggot,

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.230
they change colors.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.020
So when you actually see
the maggots in "Daybreak,"

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.320
you'll see that there's a
range going from translucent

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.440
to white and then leaning
in towards a beige

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.140
and then to a brown.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.890
Narrator: First she added coconut pudding,

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.270
followed by caramel
coloring and almond milk

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.270
to make them darker as they aged.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:22.920
Wait, let me also act like
I'm actually eating maggots.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.855
If I was on "Fear Factor" or something.

00:05:24.855 --> 00:05:27.605
[dramatic music]

00:05:29.648 --> 00:05:31.466
Yeah, these taste great.
Have you guys had these?

00:05:31.466 --> 00:05:32.900
They're like fruit snacks.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.347
Narrator: The little details
Melissa had to get right

00:05:35.347 --> 00:05:37.764
in each maggot pale in comparison

00:05:37.764 --> 00:05:39.253
to the cockroach from
"Crazy Ex-Girlfriend."

00:05:39.253 --> 00:05:41.980
Edible props are frequently
shown in close-up form,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:43.682
meaning Melissa couldn't get away

00:05:43.682 --> 00:05:45.160
with using a date on its own.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:49.510
It also needed legs,
antennae, wings, and a head.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.210
Chile threads replicated floppy antennae,

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.330
and dry seaweed was sturdy
enough for its legs.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.370
As for the body,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.650
Melissa sorted through an
entire container of dates.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:02.150
The first thing that I look at is the size

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.510
and to make sure that it's
not too big or too small

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:05.920
for the final cockroach.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.670
The second thing that I look for

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.980
is that they're not too wrinkled,
they're not too shriveled.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.840
Some of them are too
broken up to actually use,

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.490
since the wings need
to be all in one piece

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.070
and look like they're attached.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:20.700
The reason that I picked these
three dates to start with

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.750
is they're all about
the same uniform size,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.570
and the skins are perfectly intact.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:27.680
Narrator: This allowed her to filet

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:28.800
and flatten the dates,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.560
as she noticed that roaches
are typically flatter

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.960
than that of average dates.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.950
So, taking out as much of the meat

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.320
in the back part of the date as I can.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.610
And that leaves the wings
separate and hollow.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.360
And then the second date that we choose

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.290
is the one that I use for the head.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.150
I usually look for ones
that have a little smaller

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.130
and pointed top, and that
are a little bit darker

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:54.180
than the actual cockroach
body we've chosen.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.400
Narrator: Meanwhile, the
natural stickiness of the dates

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.300
acted as a glue when
piecing the two together.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.180
Making sure everything fit together

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.830
was why choosing the exact
right pieces of seaweed

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.530
was so crucial.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:09.420
It's just looking for ones
that have a slight curve to it

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.870
so that they can actually
fit into the body

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.550
and leave the legs straight.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:14.610
Narrator: The monotonous task

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:17.510
of choosing the right
strands pays off on camera.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:20.188
In the final shot, the
flexible chili threads

00:07:20.188 --> 00:07:23.740
naturally move with the wind
like real antennae would.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.940
Plus, they add the crunch
missing from the date.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.257
It's so nice, I don't want to ruin it.

00:07:28.257 --> 00:07:29.663
But got to do it.

00:07:37.846 --> 00:07:42.160
The seaweed also gives it a
surprise salty fishiness to it,

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.830
so even that  reaction
was a bit authentic.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.783
'cause I was like, well, I
know what dates taste like,

00:07:46.783 --> 00:07:47.810
and then when I had this salty,

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.940
fishy taste of seaweed, I'm like, "Uh-oh."

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.360
Narrator: It's these minute details

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.580
that make the most convincing edible props

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.490
and get the best reactions.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:58.210
For "Judy Moody and
the Not Bummer Summer,"

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:01.320
Melissa created edible
pieces of Bigfoot's poop,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.680
which the characters accidentally eat.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.300
Using peanut butter and
chocolate for color and texture

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.200
seems obvious, but there's
more than meets the eye.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:10.480
The nuts are a nice touch.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.230
Well, it's not just
poop, it's Bigfoot poop.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.744
So we have to assume
Bigfoot lives in the woods.

00:08:16.744 --> 00:08:17.885
Joe: Yeah, wow.

00:08:17.885 --> 00:08:18.985
Melissa: He eats nuts and berries.

00:08:18.985 --> 00:08:20.625
Joe: I figured his poop
would've been a little bigger.

00:08:20.625 --> 00:08:22.450
Also would have would've accepted corn.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:28.517
Oh yeah. It's like a brownie.

00:08:28.517 --> 00:08:29.884
It is a brownie, right?

00:08:29.884 --> 00:08:30.967
Kind of like.

00:08:33.871 --> 00:08:35.830
Mentally, I'm just being
like -- it's still like --

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:36.888
this looks a lot like poop.

00:08:36.888 --> 00:08:37.871
poop, poop, poop, poop, poop,

00:08:37.871 --> 00:08:39.478
its going on your mouth,
stop putting it in.

00:08:39.478 --> 00:08:41.109
I'm like, "Mmm, supper."

00:08:41.109 --> 00:08:43.170
Oh my God, this piece of
poop ain't going to hold me.

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:47.218
I'd be like, "Ahh, mwah,
mwah, mwah!" [laughs]

00:08:47.218 --> 00:08:48.677
"This poop is fantastic."

